From 6bc2c22f9772d0c470eeb28df09c3b3e239afd5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:10:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/11] feat(registry): sign list page tokens and expose them
 through list_page

The registry could only continue a listing from a token built with the
private _encode_token, and that token was plain base64 JSON: any caller
could forge a position, and nothing tied it to the tenant or the filters
it was issued for. API pagination needs an opaque token it can safely
hand to clients.

list_page() returns the items together with next_page_token, which is
set only when more items match. A token is <payload>.<signature>; the
signature is an HMAC-SHA256 over the payload plus tenant, kind,
namespace and label selector, keyed by a random secret persisted in a
new registry_meta table so tokens survive restarts. The limit is
deliberately not bound, so clients may change the page size between
pages. Malformed, modified or foreign tokens raise
InvalidPageTokenError (a ValueError). list() delegates to list_page(),
so its continue_token must now come from list_page().
